# Edit original files only

When changing behavior in this project:

- **Modify the original source file in place** (the real `.rs` / `.py` / `.cc` / `.toml` that the build uses).
- **Do not** deliver behavior by adding parallel “patch”, “overlay”, “sidecar”, or “sew-together” files that the build does not own.
- **Do not** leave half-applied edits across duplicate copies (`upgrade_backup/`, `*_backup/`, generated patch dumps) as the source of truth.
- Prefer `ApplyPatch` / direct edits on the canonical path under `rerun/`, `tools/`, `scripts/`, etc.
- One-off scripts that rewrite files are allowed only if they **write the final content into the original file** and are discarded afterward — the lasting change must live in the original.
- If a feature needs a new module, add it as a **normal project module** wired from `mod.rs` / `entrypoint` / CMake/Bazel — not as an external patch stack.

Violations to avoid: Python “stitch” scripts that splice fragments into files without leaving a clean original; documenting “apply this patch later”; keeping two divergent copies and merging by hand each build.
